---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import { ESSAYS, HOW_PATH, SITE_LAB, adrUrl, howUrl, labUrl } from '../../lib/links';

// The essay the Cache Lab maps to (docs/domain.md, "The principle map"; Q33 · Q-principles). Static,
// no island. Every claim about Cloudflare's behaviour is as its documentation stated it on the
// date in READ, and every number from the code names its file. What the lab shows is the lab page
// as read that day.
const READ = '2026-11-06';
const { title } = ESSAYS.cache;
const SOURCES = [
  {
    href: 'https://developers.cloudflare.com/workers/runtime-apis/cache/',
    title: 'Cache',
    by: 'Cloudflare’s Workers documentation',
    for: 'the contents of the cache do not replicate outside the data center they were written in, and a delete purges only that data center',
  },
  {
    href: 'https://developers.cloudflare.com/workers/reference/how-the-cache-works/',
    title: 'How the Cache works',
    by: 'Cloudflare’s Workers documentation',
    for: 'the Cache API is local to a data center and does not support tiered caching',
  },
  {
    href: 'https://developers.cloudflare.com/workers/cache/',
    title: 'Workers Cache',
    by: 'Cloudflare’s Workers documentation',
    for: 'with caching enabled, every request to the Worker is charged at the standard request rate, static asset requests included',
  },
  {
    href: 'https://developers.cloudflare.com/workers/static-assets/billing-and-limitations/',
    title: 'Billing and Limitations',
    by: 'Cloudflare’s documentation on static assets',
    for: 'requests to static assets are free and unlimited',
  },
  {
    href: 'https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9110.html',
    title: 'RFC 9110, HTTP Semantics',
    by: 'IETF, 2022',
    for: 'ETag, If-None-Match and 304 Not Modified',
  },
  {
    href: 'https://www.rfc-editor.org/rfc/rfc9111.html',
    title: 'RFC 9111, HTTP Caching',
    by: 'IETF, 2022',
    for: 'what private and no-cache ask of a browser’s cache',
  },
  {
    href: labUrl('Cache Lab') as string,
    title: 'The Cache Lab',
    by: 'the Cache Lab on ihaveasegway.com',
    for: 'a cached menu expiring under load, with coalescing, stale serving, early refresh and invalidation on write, simulated in your browser',
  },
];
---

<Base
  title={`${title} | Proof of Concept Bakery`}
  description="How Proof of Concept Bakery caches each sandbox's menu under a key that names its version, why nothing is ever invalidated, what the ETag adds, and what the cache does not save."
>
  <article class="essay prose">
    <p class="hint">
      <a href={HOW_PATH}>How it’s built</a>
    </p>
    <h1>{title}</h1>
    <p class="lead">
      The menu is read far more often than it changes. The bakery caches it under a key that names
      its version, so a new price is served from the first read after it is published and an old one
      is never served again, with no expiry to wait for and nothing to delete. This is how, what it
      leaves out, and what it costs.
    </p>

    <h2>A price, and when people see it</h2>
    <p>
      The seed’s menu has 24 items, and the country loaf costs $9.50 (
      <code>crates/seed/src/menu.rs</code>). A member of staff in the console sets it to $10.00.
      That is a draft: the Catalog object, one per sandbox, holds it as pending, and every reader
      still gets $9.50. Pressing Publish makes the drafts the menu, adds one to the menu’s version
      and records <code>MenuPublished</code>. From the next read on, everyone gets $10.00. The
      question for a cache is what happens between those two moments, and the bakery’s answer is
      that nothing does: there is no between.
    </p>

    <h2>What the Cache Lab shows, and what it cannot</h2>
    <p>
      The{' '}
      <a href={labUrl('Cache Lab')} rel="noopener">
        Cache Lab
      </a>
      , as read on {READ}, sends about 300 reads a second at a menu held in four cache nodes in
      front of a database with eight connections. Every entry has a time to live, and when the
      menu’s runs out the readers pile onto the database at once. Its switches are the usual
      answers: coalesce the misses, serve the stale copy while one fetch refreshes it, refresh
      early, jitter the expiry. Its writes either invalidate the entry or leave it, and it asks you
      to count the readers who get the old version until it expires.
    </p>
    <p>
      The lab has no versioned keys and no ETag, and its cache is one place. Each of those is where
      the bakery differs, and the third is where the real platform differs from any simulation of
      one cache.
    </p>

    <h2>How a read works</h2>
    <p>
      <code>GET /v1/sandboxes/…/menu</code> (<code>workers/api/src/http/menu.rs</code>) first asks
      the Catalog for its version, a small read that the one object answering it makes strongly
      consistent: a publish that has committed is the version the next read sees. Then:
    </p>
    <ul>
      <li>
        If the browser sent <code>If-None-Match</code> with the ETag of that version, such as{' '}
        <code>"v2"</code>, the answer is 304 with no body, and nothing else is asked.
      </li>
      <li>
        Otherwise the Worker looks in the Cache API under{' '}
        <code>&lt;API origin&gt;/_cache/menu/&lt;sandbox&gt;/v&lt;version&gt;</code>, a URL no route
        answers. A hit is served with <code>Bakery-Cache: hit</code>.
      </li>
      <li>
        A miss asks the Catalog for the menu, stores it under the key of the version the body itself
        carries, in case a publish landed between the two reads, and serves it with{' '}
        <code>Bakery-Cache: miss</code>.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Nothing to invalidate</h2>
    <p>
      Invalidation is the work of finding every copy of something that changed and removing it in
      time. With the version in the key there is no such work. After the publish, every read asks
      for <code>v2</code>; the entry for <code>v1</code> is still in the cache and is never asked
      for again. Correctness does not depend on anything being deleted, so nothing has to be deleted
      on time, and a delete that fails costs nothing but space.
    </p>
    <p>
      The bakery does delete the old entry, as housekeeping: the consumer of{' '}
      <code>MenuPublished</code> removes the previous version’s key (
      <code>workers/api/src/queue.rs</code>). Cloudflare’s documentation, read on {READ}, says a
      delete purges only the data center the Worker ran in, so this frees the space where the
      consumer happened to run and nowhere else. Elsewhere the old entry ages out on its own: the
      code stores every entry with <code>max-age=86400</code>, a day, because no sandbox outlives
      the next 10:00 UTC. That is the only time-based rule in the menu’s cache, and it decides when
      space is freed, never what a reader sees.
    </p>

    <h2>The browser’s cache has a rule of its own</h2>
    <p>
      The menu’s answer carries <code>Cache-Control: private, no-cache</code>, the one route allowed
      something other than <code>no-store</code> (<code>workers/api/src/http/headers.rs</code>).
      Under RFC 9111 that lets the browser keep its copy, and only its own, but never use it without
      asking first. The menu screen sends the ETag back each time, and an unchanged menu costs a 304
      and no body. That is a second cache with a second key: the Cache API is keyed by the version
      on the server, the browser’s by the ETag it holds, and both are the same number. A 304 carries
      no <code>Bakery-Cache</code> header, because the Cache API was never asked.
    </p>

    <h2>One miss for every data center</h2>
    <p>
      Cloudflare’s pages, read on {READ}, are plain that the Cache API is local to a data center:
      what one stores does not replicate outside it, and the Cache API does not use tiered caching.
      So <code>v2</code> misses once in every data center that serves the sandbox, not once in the
      world. A visitor’s requests usually reach one data center, so in practice it is one miss a
      publish. Two reads that miss at the same moment in one data center both build the menu,
      because nothing coalesces them; at one visitor’s traffic that is rare and cheap.
    </p>

    <h2>What the bakery does not use</h2>
    <p>
      No Workers Cache on <code>bakery-web</code>, the Worker that serves the storefront’s pages. As
      Cloudflare’s documentation stated it on {READ}, requests to static assets are free, and with
      Workers Cache on, every request is billed at the standard rate, static assets included. CI
      fails any <code>wrangler.jsonc</code> that names the key (
      <code>.github/workflows/ci.yml</code>). The public <code>/menu/</code> page, before a sandbox
      exists, is the seed’s menu built into the HTML, so it costs nothing to serve and has nothing
      to cache. And no expiry on the menu besides the day-long <code>max-age</code>: a time to live
      is how a cache guesses when its copy has gone stale, and a key that names the version never
      has to guess.
    </p>

    <h2>What it costs</h2>
    <ul>
      <li>
        <strong>Every read still asks the Catalog.</strong> The version check is a Durable Object
        request on every read, hit or miss, and a miss adds the menu read. What the cache saves is
        that second request and the menu’s body, not the first; the first is the price of never
        serving an old menu. The bakery’s cost model (<code>docs/cost.md</code>, as of 2026-11-04)
        prices Durable Object requests at 1,000,000 a month included on Paid, then $0.15 a million.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>One miss a publish, per data center.</strong> Each publish makes the next read in
        each data center build the menu again.
      </li>
      <li>
        <strong>A new price nobody sees until it is published.</strong> A draft lives in the
        Catalog, and the menu a visitor reads is only ever a published version.
      </li>
    </ul>

    <h2>Try it</h2>
    <p>
      Open <a href="/menu/">the menu</a> with a sandbox: its “How this works” line names the
      version, the <code>Bakery-Cache</code> header and the ETag. Press Ask again and the answer is
      a 304. Sign in, change a price in <a href="/app/">the console</a>, and ask again: still the
      old version, since a draft is not the menu. Publish it and ask once more: a new version, a new
      ETag and <code>Bakery-Cache: miss</code>, then a hit, or a 304, from then on.
    </p>

    <h2>Sources</h2>
    <ul class="sources">
      {SOURCES.map((s) => (
        <li>
          <a href={s.href} rel="noopener">
            {s.title}
          </a>
          , {s.by}, read on {READ}: {s.for}.
        </li>
      ))}
    </ul>
    <p class="hint">
      Back to <a href={howUrl('cache')}>the Cache principle on How it’s built</a>, to the decision
      that chose it,{' '}
      <a href={adrUrl('0003')} rel="noopener">
        ADR 0003
      </a>
      , or to{' '}
      <a href={SITE_LAB} rel="noopener">
        the lab
      </a>
      .
    </p>
  </article>
</Base>
